{
  "dom_id": "step-42308",
  "name": "Expectation and Variance of Slope Parameter Estimates",
  "problem": "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy},$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}\\cdot 0+{\\beta }_{1}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n\n- For the variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
